WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:10.830
So while you guys were gone, I went on Wikipedia
and looked up Osiris, because when I did the

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:15.809
research for this book it certainly wasn’t
possible to get access to that kind of information

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:20.940
instantaneously. And the stories that are
laid out in there… there’s a thread that

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.950
runs the stories that are laid out there,
that’s quite similar to the one I told you,

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:29.790
but there’s all sorts of details that are
very radically different. And so, I don’t

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:34.510
really know what to say about that. And you
know, part of the reason that people like

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:41.570
Joseph Campbell and Carl Jung and… there
was an original anthropologist, an armchair

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:46.610
anthropologist, who wrote a book called The
Golden Bough, whose name escapes me momentarily

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.379
[it’s Sir James George Frazer], but it was
one of the first attempts to do a cross-cultural

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:54.910
categorisation of mythology, and to look for
underlying commonalities. Now the problem

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.550
is, you know I told you about Naomi Klein
going down to Argentina and looking at the

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:04.780
factory through her particular lens. The problem
is that when you develop a lens and then you

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.250
look at things through it, you may just see
what that lens heightens, you know, and I

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:14.390
think that’s a criticism that could be leveled
against the sorts of things that I’m telling

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:18.890
you about. You know, that I’ve cherry-picked
certain stories and interpreted them in certain

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:25.860
ways so that they fall coherently into a scheme
of thought. And so I’ll tell you some of

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:31.479
the things that I do methodologically to protect
myself against that, insofar as that’s possible.

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:39.799
So there is this idea in psychology called
the multimethod-multitrait method of construct

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:46.869
validation, which is, you know, a God-awful
agglomeration of jargon. But it actually means

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:53.090
something. And it was the construction of
two psychologists back in the late 1950s,

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:58.200
Cronbach and Meehl, who were interested in
what psychologists meant when they said something

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:05.350
existed. Like say, anger. Is that a real thing?
Well what do you mean, ‘thing’? What is

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.369
it that you’re talking about? What is anger,
exactly? I don’t mean as ‘anger’, but

00:02:09.369 --> 00:02:15.159
as an idea. What sort of idea is the idea
of anger or the idea of anxiety or the idea

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:20.620
of sadness? Or the idea of self-esteem or
self-competence or any of these things that

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:27.319
psychologists talk about as if they’re realities.
Well, some realities are very tangible, right,

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:33.121
and it’s somewhat easy to come to agreement
about the contours of their existence, and

00:02:33.121 --> 00:02:39.950
the borders, but other things are way more
difficult than that. And so their idea was

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.409
that if something actually existed, you should
be able to measure it multiple ways, using

00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:51.010
multiple techniques, and still get the same
answer. And so, your senses do that. You have

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.430
five of them. And the reason you have five,
apparently, is because evolution has determined

00:02:55.430 --> 00:03:00.990
that if you only had four you’d die more
often. So it seems as if you have five separate

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:07.459
sources of information that all lead to the
same conclusion. You can be relatively certain

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:14.670
that the conclusion’s that reached is real.
Well, the multimethod-multitrait method is

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:20.680
an application of that idea to construct validation,
which is like… you could think of anger

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:26.489
as an idea, as a construct, it’s a terminological
issue. You just define it as a construct,

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:31.069
a psychological construct. How do you know
if they’re real? Well the idea is it’s

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:35.890
very complicated. You have to measure them
in multiple ways. All the ways you measure

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:42.650
them have to give the same reading. Then you
have to show that that idea is the same in

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:47.400
certain ways as other ideas that it’s related
to and different from other ideas that it’s

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:54.920
not related. So let me give an example. There’s
a construct: ‘neuroticism’. And you might

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:00.020
say, well there’s another construct: ‘depression’.
Well then you might say, “well are those

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:07.380
separate or are they the same?” Well that’s
a tough one, because in many, many ways they’re

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:13.350
the same. Are they different in any important
ways? Well, the way you do that technically

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:18.350
is you would measure neuroticism however you
were going to measure it. Maybe I’d have

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.280
you tell me how neurotic you were using a
questionnaire, and then I’d have your friend

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.270
tell me and maybe your parent, so we’ve
got a pretty good triangulation on your neuroticism.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:32.690
Then I have them do the same thing, all those
people do the same thing with regards to your

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:38.370
levels of depression and anxiety. Then I’d
use both those measures to predict something

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:47.360
else, like how well your life goes in terms
of career over the next ten years. And if

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.030
neuroticism predicted and depression predicted,
so they were correlated, then I’d think

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:56.280
well there’s some relationship. Then I’d
look at them at the same time. If I predict

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:03.750
with neuroticism and then I add depression
to the mix, does that give me more predictive

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:10.750
power. It’s sort of like does A = C or does
A + B = C? That’s all you’re asking in

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:18.380
a regression equation, roughly speaking. Well
if A = C is just as good as A + B = C, especially

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:24.190
if the two things are also correlated (depression/anxiety
and neuroticism are going to be very highly

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:29.340
correlated, like 0.7, something like that),
you might say well those are the same things.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:35.110
Just because they have different names, it’s
not helped. And part of it is, it’s a pragmatic

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:41.100
conceptualisation of reality, it’s like,
is this categorisation structure good for

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:45.650
something?
Okay, so anyways, I’ll unpack all that and

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:50.440
say, well part of my rules for determining
whether these things were useful or not was

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:55.580
to see if the ideas apply across multiple
levels of analysis. So that’s why I told

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:59.930
you about the conceptual, so that’s sort
of the cognitive psychology, and then the

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:07.500
neuropsychological, so there’s some input
of brain research. And then the narrative

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:12.849
or mythological, and then the cross-cultural
and think well those all stack up in a certain

00:06:12.849 --> 00:06:17.410
way so that if you look between all of them
you can see a pattern. And it seems to me

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:23.380
increasingly unlikely that that pattern would
be there across all of those levels at the

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:29.419
same time. Now you could say well, you know
that depends on how good you are at weaving

00:06:29.419 --> 00:06:38.490
a story. But at some point, encapsulating
that many phenomena and weaving a coherent

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:44.139
story out of them if such a thing doesn’t
exist, it seems to me to become cognitively

00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:50.669
impossible. You just can’t do it unless
there’s actually something there. And of

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:55.580
course everything I tell you also is a simplification.
It has to be. Because you could spend your

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:59.690
whole life investigating any of the things
we’re talking about and so what I’m hoping

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:06.380
is I’ve extracted out the central issues
of these multiple levels of analysis and put

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:15.849
together, each of them support all the others.
Anyways, who knows? You know, I don’t know,

00:07:15.849 --> 00:07:21.190
but so far so good. Okay so this is a cool
representation, I really like this one. This

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:27.800
is called The Open Virgin. And it’s a 14th
or 13th century sculpture, if I remember correctly.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:31.811
It’s made out of wood. And these things
were actually… this isn’t the only one

00:07:31.811 --> 00:07:39.240
of them. They were a form of religious interpretation
that had different exemplars. You could find

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.910
different ones of them online. This is brilliant,
this bloody thing, as far as I’m concerned.

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:52.349
So let me show you this figure again. So basically
what you have here, in this figure, this is

00:07:52.349 --> 00:07:58.069
a world conception. And the world conception
is roughly: there’s an individual, positive

00:07:58.069 --> 00:08:05.240
and negative, he or she is in a cultural sphere
that’s positive or negative, that’s embedded

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:09.570
in the world outside of culture, that’s
positive and negative, and all of it comes

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:18.090
from potential. That’s the idea.
Now, this map shows all of the positives and

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:22.210
negatives in relationship to one another,
and you see it’s not perfectly symmetrical

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:27.449
and the reason for that is, as far as I can
tell, the adversarial element of the individual

00:08:27.449 --> 00:08:32.920
is the thing that generates the tyrannical
element of culture. And that’s the thing

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:41.940
that gets in your way of establishing a creative
relationship with what you don’t know. Well,

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:51.070
anyways. Here is a manifestation of that,
I believe. And I like this manifestation because…

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:58.690
like, it’s obviously Christian, but not
exactly. It’s more mythological than Christian.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:08.140
And the reason for that, well, is quite obvious.
So the superordinate figure is Mary, the feminine.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:12.650
Now you think, well it’s amazing the person
who carved this didn’t get burnt up at the

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:18.830
Inquisition, because it’s a radical idea,
but it’s just an indication of how the mythology

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:22.920
can change the causal relationship between
these different elements.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:29.670
So you have Mary there, she’s the ultimate
in femininity, the ultimate feminine deity,

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:37.660
but only the positive side. And then within
her, so it’s like Nature opens itself up,

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:43.610
benevolent Nature opens itself up, and what
you see inside is culture, that’s God the

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:50.000
Father, holding the individual, and the individual
in this case is the crucified Christ, which

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.410
is a wonderful conceptualisation as far as
I’m concerned, because of course that is

00:09:54.410 --> 00:10:00.060
what life does to people, that’s why that’s
such a powerful symbol. It’s like, you know,

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:06.520
Christ is the person who has to face the reality
of his own death, voluntarily or involuntarily,

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:13.260
who’s betrayed by his culture, betrayed
by his friends, it’s sort of like he’s

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:21.540
exposed to the most torturous elements of
individual being. And simultaneously, at least

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:24.630
in principle, transcends them. Well it’s
like you bloody well better do that because

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:29.920
otherwise, well what’s going to happen?
You’re done if you don’t do that. And

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:35.260
it’s even worse, it’s not only that you
fail, that’s just the beginning of it. If

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:41.380
you can’t transcend the tragedy of your
being then you get corrupted. I see this happening

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:48.150
to people happening all the time.
So you know, if you’re suffering and you

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:56.880
can’t cope with it, generally speaking what
happens is that it embitters you. And once

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.900
you get bitter you get resentful, and once
you get resentful you look for revenge. And

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:07.300
that can just get completely out of hand.
Now that’s a quick trip to the worst suburb

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:13.440
of the Underworld, that’s for sure. So you
know, it’s not only that if you don’t

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:19.810
manage this transcendent act that you’re
swamped by the tragedy of your life. You’re

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:24.600
swamped by the tragedy of your life, and then
that corrupts you, and then you start working

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:30.960
to make everything even more tragic than it
has to be. And so I think that’s why in

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:38.920
medieval Christian conceptualisations in particular,
the idea of hell, the idea that you were destined

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:43.381
to hell was worse than the idea of dying.
And it’s a strange idea, you know, because

00:11:43.381 --> 00:11:47.330
you think (this is what the terror management
theorists think too) is that “there’s

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:50.960
nothing worse than the fear of death!” It’s
like, that’s wrong. They don’t have the

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:58.390
right imagination. There are lots of things
that are worse than the fear of death. I’m

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:05.520
not trying to trivialise the fear of death,
obviously, but I don’t think it’s very

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.890
difficult to understand that there are some
things that you would rather die than do.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:16.440
I mean I’m sure that I could get you guys
to sit for fifteen minutes and say okay, list

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:22.250
ten things that you would rather die than
do. That’s easy. Especially if you’re

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:25.930
a parent. You can think of those things in
like no time flat.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:30.930
Okay so that’s pretty interesting. So you’ve
got the superordinate feminine figure here,

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:34.830
which is not what you would expect from a
Christian representation, right? You’d expect

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:38.750
God to be on the outside, and then Mary and
then Christ, in all likelihood, although that

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:45.300
would also be a valid representation. Because
you can think of the mother as something that’s

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:50.550
protected by the culture. And supporting the
individual, because that’s basically what

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:56.340
this representation shows. But then you see
on the open sides, I really like this, I think

00:12:56.340 --> 00:13:01.270
it’s so cool, is that you’ve got all these
people who aren’t archetypal figures, they’re

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:06.210
in a little crowd. And they’re gazing at
the central figure. And of course that’s

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:09.620
exactly what’s happened in Christianity
for two thousand years, right, insofar as

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:14.210
it has spread, is that for two thousand years
people have been looking at that image, which

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:23.290
is the crucifixion, transfixed by it. Well
why? Well, you know, for reasons that we don’t

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:29.410
understand. When everybody woke up in the
late 1800s and said, “why were we looking

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:34.060
at that for the last two thousand years?”
the first answer was “well we really don’t

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:39.740
have the foggiest idea”. It was just one
of the things that we did, roughly speaking.

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:45.390
It encapsulated people within the belief system.
So that’s another good example, anyway,

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:50.420
of how this archetypal imagery can manifest
itself, say spontaneously, in the imagination

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:57.870
of an artist. And the thing is, that’s a
sacred image. And it is an actuality, that’s

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:04.920
what it is. What it shows is, well there’s
a superordinate, transcendent, natural order

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:09.540
that has a beneficial element that we associate
with femininity, and inside that there’s

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:14.621
a supportive culture that’s properly nested
if things are organised properly. And it’s

00:14:14.621 --> 00:14:19.520
both of those things that provide support
for the suffering individuals. It’s like

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:26.290
yeah, that’s pretty much got it. Now that
doesn’t exhaust the meaning of this particular

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:32.370
representation, there’s a lot more to it
than that, but just that is not so bad.

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:38.120
And then, you know I can show you a more negative
representation, I think I’ve shown you this

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:44.990
before. Like this would be a representation
of the negative element of the feminine. And

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:51.880
so you have Kali here, and Kali is the goddess
to whom you make sacrifices and hopefully

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:57.320
if you make the right sacrifices, then Kali
turns into her positive counterpart. And you

00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:03.990
know, you think, well that’s a… what…
how does sacrifice affect your relationship

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:07.260
with the gods? Well first of all there’s
no gods and second of all, well then what

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:11.960
the hell are you doing with the sacrifices?
But it’s pretty straightforward. We’ve

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:17.850
already talked about this at length. You do
make sacrifices, all the time. And what that

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:24.420
means is that you act as if you can have a
relationship with being. You assume that if

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:29.890
you withhold certain things from yourself
now, that you will improve your relationship,

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:35.250
both with nature and with culture. And I already
told you guys that as far as I was concerned

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:42.040
that was the most stunning cognitive discovery
of mankind. It’s the discovery of the future.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:46.230
And it’s the discovery of the relationship
between the present and the future. You know

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:50.762
it’s such an amazing idea, that you can
alter your behaviour now, and it’ll pay

00:15:50.762 --> 00:15:56.860
off in a year. God, that’s not obvious.
It’s certainly not… animals don’t think

00:15:56.860 --> 00:16:01.520
that way. You know, some of them act that
way, like bees and so on, but that’s just

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:05.240
built right into their behaviour, as far as
we can tell.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:11.910
So anyway, so there’s Kali. And you know,
she’s a spider. She’s in a web of fire.

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:18.120
The fire is outside a web that’s made out
of skulls. She’s got weapons in her hand,

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:22.610
her hair is on fire. She’s got a headdress
of skulls. I mean, if you ever saw something

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:25.800
like that it would traumatise you so badly
you wouldn’t be able to get out of your

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:30.790
house for like five years. That’s exactly
what it is. That’s what it is. It’s a

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:37.430
representation of all of that that would traumatise
you if you encountered it. And you know, it’s

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:41.610
an attempt to encapsulate that as an image,
and what you could say, in some sense, is

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:47.500
that the people who developed this image were
attempting to conceptualise the class of all

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:53.200
terrifying things. And once you get the class
of all terrifying things conceptualised, you

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:58.330
could come up with the class of all useful
responses. And that would be something like

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:05.200
the proper way to behave, and sacrifice is
an integral element of that. It’s like yeah,

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:10.480
absolutely. Absolutely dead on. Unless you
don’t believe that sacrifice works, in which

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:15.160
case, what the hell are you doing here? I
mean, you know, you act out your belief. And

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:20.440
it’s a firm belief. And you would also say
that if things fell apart so badly that sacrifice

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:26.020
no longer paid off, your life would basically
become intolerable. Because it would mean

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:31.940
that you would have abandoned all sense of
control over the outcomes of your actions.

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:40.050
Well you can’t live like that, you’d just
die of stress. Well and as I said, she was

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:44.440
pregnant, she’s given birth to this unfortunate
character, who she’s devouring intestines

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:50.250
first. Like it’s a rough image. It’s an
image of the destructive element of nature.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:55.750
And so that’s the negative side of the feminine,
we might say.

00:17:55.750 --> 00:18:09.450
There’s a positive representation. This
is a Greek representation. That’s Diana,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:18.270
or Artemis. I’m hoping I’m remembering
this properly. She’s multi-breasted. This

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:26.820
circle around her head is undoubtedly a representation
of her cosmic nature. So it’s like a halo

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:34.430
but the halo is basically a planetary body
like the Sun… a cosmic body like the Sun

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:40.000
or the Moon behind the head of the divine
individual with all of that power shining

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:44.440
forward. So it’s an attempt to use… you
know, if you look up at the Moon or you look

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:49.360
up at the Sun, there’s an element that’s
characterised by awe. You can’t help but

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:54.820
feel that, at least its beauty and power,
and sometimes it’s overwhelming. Well, you

00:18:54.820 --> 00:19:00.340
take the representation of the personality
and you throw the representation of these

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:07.030
cosmic powers behind it, and it’s an attempt
to represent transcendent value. It’s very

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:12.290
intelligent. It’s brilliant, in fact. And
so partly what you’re trying to do as you

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:17.620
conduct your life is you’re trying to get
all of these archetypal forces in proper balance.

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:24.610
And I suppose in some sense to get them tilted
towards the good, if that’s possible. I

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:38.210
mean, that’s what this representation in
some sense, you might say… [looks for picture]…

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:45.490
yeah, you might say well if you got everything
ordered properly, this is what being would

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:53.170
be like. Still pretty rough, given that the
crucifix is at its centre.

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:59.620
Now I don’t know, you know, if you think
about it from a purely technical perspective,

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:04.990
roughly speaking, each of these archetypes
is split into positive and negative. You might

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:12.870
say, well, that’s just how it is. There
is no way that you can interact with all of

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:21.220
them so that the whole system tilts towards
the positive, you know. But I don’t think

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:26.020
that’s true. I think you can get them lined
up so that things tilt towards the positive.

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:30.760
And I think you can detect when that happens,
because those are the periods in your life

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:35.760
where you feel that you’re richly enough
engaged in what you’re doing and life is

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:42.280
meaningful enough, so that the rest of it,
the substructure that makes it necessary,

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:48.050
is worth it. It’s worthwhile, you know,
that the negative exists is okay given the

00:20:48.050 --> 00:20:53.820
way that things are structured.
Now I think that whether or not that’s true

00:20:53.820 --> 00:21:02.620
is in some sense the eternal question of mankind.
Can you organise your life so that it’s

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:09.660
good? Well, it seems like you can certainly
allow your life to degenerate so that it’s

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:13.430
terrible. That seems indisputable, right,
and there does seem to be an element of choice

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:19.850
in that, although, you know something terrible
can happen to you that you have no choice

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:25.300
of or control over. But it certainly seems
that you could do any number of stupid and

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:30.060
blind things that make everything worse. I
think that’s common experience. And I also

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:35.160
think you’d be hard-pressed to find anyone
who wouldn’t say that there were things

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:41.131
they did consciously and willingly, that were
of their own choice, that they didn’t have

00:21:41.131 --> 00:21:48.150
to do, that made things worse. So we seem
to have some sense of our moral agency. And

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:53.480
we seem automatically to accept some responsibility
for the outcomes of our actions. And so partly

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:57.650
what that means is that we do believe that
we can make things better or worse. And I

00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:02.820
suppose in some way that means that we believe
we can tap the whole archetypal structure

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:16.910
of being into the positive. Well I would say
if you drill down to the bottom of existentialism,

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:21.670
in some sense, that’s the question that
the existentialists pose, you know. Is there

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:27.280
a manner of conducting your life such that
it becomes… it’s not merely acceptable,

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:35.930
it’s more than that. You’d say yes, this
is how it should be.

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:39.670
What we’re going to do now, although we’re
not going to do it today, is we’re going

00:22:39.670 --> 00:22:45.020
to start talking about other representations
of this sub-structure from other religious

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:50.390
traditions. And we’re going to also talk
about ways that you might relate to these

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:56.200
figures. Because one question might be, well
you’re part of your culture, and it’s

00:22:56.200 --> 00:23:01.630
got an orderly element, and a protective element,
and a tyrannical element. Okay so what should

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:08.530
you do about that? You know, should you…
the tyrannical element is real and it’s

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:12.840
corrupt and blind, and so to the degree that
you identify with that, when you’re tyrannical

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:17.880
and corrupt and blind, but by the same token
you’re the fortunate beneficiary of tens

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:25.330
of thousands of years of cultural and technological
evolution and effort. And it seems to me that,

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:30.521
you know, you should be at least appropriately
grateful for that. And so then you’re in

00:23:30.521 --> 00:23:35.530
a real position, because you have to be grateful
and happy for the thing that tyrannises and

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:40.900
controls you. Well, okay, that’s not an
easy thing to figure out, because those things

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:47.770
seem to conflict. So what exactly should your
relationship to culture be? Well, I think

00:23:47.770 --> 00:23:54.830
there are myths that describe that quite directly.
And they take into account the corruption.

00:23:54.830 --> 00:24:02.440
It’s not a whitewash. It’s like yes of
course this thing is dead and blind and corrupt.

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:10.340
Obviously. And you know, it’s foolishness
and wilfulness… it’s ignorance and wilful

00:24:10.340 --> 00:24:15.690
blindness that make it that way, as well as
things merely getting old and falling apart.

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:19.740
So the fact that there’s some malevolence
at the bottom of it is also true. Should you

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:27.020
flee from that? Well, that isn’t what the
myths say. They say what you’re supposed

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:31.460
to do is, just like Pinocchio, you’re supposed
to go to the bottom of the ocean, to the most

00:24:31.460 --> 00:24:36.890
monstrous possible part, and rescue your father.
That’s what you have to do before you become

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:44.410
an individual.
And I can’t see how that can’t be right.

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:50.040
In fact, I think that’s what you’re doing
in university, if you’re actually getting

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:55.840
a university education. What you’re here
to do is sift through the dead remnants of

00:24:55.840 --> 00:25:01.770
the past and pull up what’s vital, that’s
so sophisticated and complex that there’s

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:05.570
no way that you’d be able to think it up
for yourself. But you know, it requires you

00:25:05.570 --> 00:25:11.120
as the discriminating agent. You know, if
you read someone like Nietzsche or you read

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:15.960
someone like Freud, there’s lots to throw
away. It isn’t what you throw away though,

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:20.570
it’s what you keep that matters. And to
be a discriminating reader means that you

00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:26.260
get rid of the dead matter and you keep the
gold. And you build yourself up a storehouse

00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:34.440
of gold, and then you can live in the world.
And people often ask, what’s the purpose

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:40.270
of getting a degree in the humanities? Well
the answer to that is that if you’re properly

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:47.309
trained in the humanities, you know how to
live. Now that’s the same as having a job,

00:25:47.309 --> 00:25:53.040
and you need to have a job. But you know,
knowing how to live is a more important thing

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:58.890
than having a job, even though having a job
is an important thing. And the reason for

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:04.770
that is that if you don’t know how to live
then things fall apart. And that’s not good.

00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:09.930
And they can really fall apart, you know,
and part of the reason I think that this became

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:17.290
so much of an obsession for me is that I believe
that we don’t have a lottery of… we are

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:29.300
narrowing… we’ve become so powerful that
the consequence of our blindness is reaching

00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:33.890
uncontrollable proportions. If you’re weak
and stupid, well you know, there’s only

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:40.880
so much damage you can do. But we’re not
weak, not at all. We have unbelievable power

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:46.050
at our disposal now, and a lot of weird things
are going to come down the pipes in the next

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:52.070
ten years.
The more people that have their act together,

00:26:52.070 --> 00:27:00.750
the better. Because then things will be tilted
towards everything lining up in a positive

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:05.531
direction rather than being tilted in the
other direction. And that’s important. And

00:27:05.531 --> 00:27:10.770
I think people can make a very powerful individual
contribution to that, wherever you happen

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:21.679
to be. You know, all of these myths always
say the same thing. They say that the future

00:27:21.679 --> 00:27:31.160
of the world depends on individual choice.
And I think that’s literally true. So then

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:35.090
you might think, well you better put yourself
in a position where you make wise choices,

00:27:35.090 --> 00:27:43.240
because if you don’t… we’ve already
seen what happens. We got a taste in the 20th

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:51.890
century. So, if you want some more of that,
you could certainly have it.

